1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,937
This is the actual three dimensional model of our first component: the copper cooling body, revision A.

2
00:00:07,937 --> 00:00:12,703
It is seventy millimetres in diameter and seven millimetres thick.

3
00:00:12,703 --> 00:00:20,501
We will look at the target face, water channel, seals, and fasteners, and explain why each feature is there.

4
00:00:20,501 --> 00:00:25,700
The geometry is modeled; its operating performance is not yet validated.

5
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:30,764
This flat front face supports the two inch target.

6
00:00:30,764 --> 00:00:39,839
The circular outline marks its fifty point eight millimetre footprint; it is an explanatory overlay, not another modeled component.

7
00:00:39,839 --> 00:00:46,212
Heat must cross the contact between target and copper, so flatness and even clamping matter.

8
00:00:46,212 --> 00:00:48,636
We have left target thickness open.

9
00:00:48,636 --> 00:00:52,100
The target itself does not seal the cooling water.

10
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:59,844
The copper body extends nine point six millimetres radially beyond the nominal target edge.

11
00:00:59,844 --> 00:01:05,245
That gives us space to develop the clamp, fasteners, and surrounding interfaces.

12
00:01:05,245 --> 00:01:10,646
Seventy millimetres is a packaging choice, not an optimized or minimum diameter.

13
00:01:10,646 --> 00:01:17,194
Keeping the target seat flat avoids locking this first revision to a close fitting target pocket.

14
00:01:17,194 --> 00:01:20,300
The future clamp must still locate the target.

15
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:23,402
Now turn to the rear.

16
00:01:23,402 --> 00:01:27,193
The blue region is the actual machined water channel.

17
00:01:27,193 --> 00:01:35,920
It spans radii of eleven to twenty five millimetres and is three millimetres deep, with rounded seven millimetre end caps.

18
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:42,645
Water is intended to enter at one end, sweep around behind the target, and leave at the other.

19
00:01:42,645 --> 00:01:47,723
The moving markers show intended direction only, not a flow simulation.

20
00:01:47,723 --> 00:01:51,300
The ports belong in the next component: the cover.

21
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:58,954
The solid bridge between the channel ends separates the intended inlet and outlet positions.

22
00:01:58,954 --> 00:02:04,284
Without it, water could take a short route instead of sweeping around the target.

23
00:02:04,284 --> 00:02:09,746
But metal contact against the future cover does not automatically seal that bridge.

24
00:02:09,746 --> 00:02:14,023
We must design the cover and divider interface to control bypass.

25
00:02:14,023 --> 00:02:18,300
The two circular O rings provide containment, not a divider seal.

26
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:26,292
This cutaway exposes the four millimetre copper wall between the channel floor and target seat.

27
00:02:26,292 --> 00:02:32,617
It keeps water separate from the vacuum beneath the target and conducts heat into the coolant.

28
00:02:32,617 --> 00:02:40,086
A thinner wall brings the magnets closer, but increases concerns about pressure deflection and face distortion.

29
00:02:40,086 --> 00:02:42,778
A thicker wall increases magnet spacing.

30
00:02:42,778 --> 00:02:48,833
Four millimetres is our first iteration, pending structural, thermal, and magnetic checks.

31
00:02:50,433 --> 00:02:57,241
The two highlighted circular grooves accept the inner and outer face seals against the future cover.

32
00:02:57,241 --> 00:03:05,002
Candidate rings have fourteen and fifty four millimetre inside diameters, both with two millimetre cross sections.

33
00:03:05,002 --> 00:03:09,768
Each groove is two point six millimetres wide and one point five deep.

34
00:03:09,768 --> 00:03:17,257
A cover seated on the lands gives twenty five percent nominal squeeze and about eighty one percent gland fill.

35
00:03:17,257 --> 00:03:19,571
These are nominal geometry values.

36
00:03:19,571 --> 00:03:25,767
Seal material, tolerances, swelling, temperature, and cover deflection still need checking.

37
00:03:27,367 --> 00:03:31,266
These six rear holes are for fastening the coolant cover.

38
00:03:31,266 --> 00:03:37,354
They are equally spaced on a sixty four millimetre bolt circle, outside the outer O ring.

39
00:03:37,354 --> 00:03:44,810
The model shows two point five millimetre pilot pockets, four millimetres deep, for intended M three threads.

40
00:03:44,810 --> 00:03:48,230
Finished threads and drill points are not modeled.

41
00:03:48,230 --> 00:03:54,934
Copper thread strength, useful engagement, and tightening load must be checked before manufacture.

42
00:03:54,934 --> 00:03:58,833
These screws retain the cover; the O rings make the seal.

43
00:04:00,433 --> 00:04:04,803
On the front are three separate pilot pockets for the target clamp.

44
00:04:04,803 --> 00:04:11,651
They sit on the same bolt circle but are rotated between the rear holes, so the two patterns do not meet.

45
00:04:11,651 --> 00:04:14,325
Their modeled depth is three millimetres.

46
00:04:14,325 --> 00:04:21,761
Separating the clamp fasteners from the coolant cover lets us change the target without opening the water circuit.

47
00:04:21,761 --> 00:04:27,500
The clamp itself, thread details, and target locating features are still to be designed.

48
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:36,851
This revision establishes the target seat, cooling passage, seal grooves, and separate fastening interfaces.

49
00:04:36,851 --> 00:04:42,593
The native part is one solid, and its volume matches an independent calculation.

50
00:04:42,593 --> 00:04:48,048
It does not yet form a closed water circuit or an atmospheric magnet cavity.

51
00:04:48,048 --> 00:04:53,646
The next part is the coolant cover, including the ports and divider treatment.

52
00:04:53,646 --> 00:05:01,900
We still need pressure, leak, thermal, magnetic, and electrical verification before this becomes a build ready gun.
